## 5. Count variance from three and four selected events

The full count cannot generally be assigned Bernoulli variance. A useful bound is nevertheless available using only the local selected-mark intensities and the full factorial kernels, independently of the provisional locality claim.

Let `mu=E C_jl`, and keep j distinct from l. In `C_jl(C_jl-1)`, two distinct counted pairs either share their first j, share their last l, or use four distinct events. A first j cannot be the last l of another pair because their labels differ.

For a shared first event, the two l times lie within b of the j time in I. The two orderings of the l events cancel the factor one-half from their ordered simplex. Their total contribution is at most

    lambda_j lambda_l^2 h_I b^2.

For a shared last event at v, both j times lie in `I intersect (v-b,v)`. If `d(v)` is the length of that interval, then `d(v)<=min(h_I,b)` and `integral d(v)dv=h_I b`. The two orderings similarly give the bound

    lambda_j^2 lambda_l integral d(v)^2 dv
       <=lambda_j^2 lambda_l h_I b min(h_I,b).

For four distinct events, keep the two pairs labeled and integrate both first times in I and both positive lags in `(0,b]`. The various chronological interlacings partition this four-variable domain. Each full four-event factorial density is bounded by `lambda_j^2 lambda_l^2`. There is no extra factorial: the ordered pair-of-pairs is already represented by the two labeled pairs. The contribution is at most

    lambda_j^2 lambda_l^2 h_I^2 b^2.

Define the sum of these three bounds to be Q. Then

    mu<=E C_jl^2<=mu+Q,
    max(0,mu-mu^2)<=Var(C_jl)<=mu+Q-mu^2.             (20)

All other marks and all intervening histories remain allowed in the factorial densities. Equation (20) has constants independent of total volume when the selected mark norms and the time windows are fixed. It is a family of finite-graph bounds, not a construction of an infinite-volume marked process.

As a fallback, (6) also gives `Var(C)<=R mu`, but this loses a graph-size factor. The sharper bound (20) is the useful rare-count result. If, as in (19), `mu_n~25 kappa^2 h_I b`, while `kappa b->0` and `kappa^2 h_I b->0`, then

    Q/mu_n=O(kappa b+kappa^2 h_I b)->0,
    Var(C_jl | preparation n)/mu_n -> 1.              (21)

Thus the variance-to-mean limit is derived from a bound on repeated contributions, not assumed from a Poisson or Bernoulli law. It does not assert that individual counts are restricted to zero or one. The independent abstract control below has a positive probability of C=4 and variance larger than its mean at finite parameters.

## 6. Repetition statement and its additional assumptions

Suppose each trial is an independent fresh realization of the declared preparation and original law. That independence/reset condition is a supplied statistical protocol, not an implemented physical apparatus or a property of one uninterrupted trajectory. Assume `A_I>0` and the controlled regime (18).

If the vacuum expectation is independently known without error at the signal scale, N one-particle trials have squared mean-difference-to-standard-error ratio

    SNR^2=N (Delta mu)^2/Var_1
       ~4 N kappa^2 b g^4 A_I^2/(25 h_I).             (22)

If both baselines are sampled with N independent trials per arm (2N trials in total),

    SNR^2=N (Delta mu)^2/(Var_1+Var_0)
       ~2 N kappa^2 b g^4 A_I^2/(25 h_I).             (23)

These statements follow from (19)-(21); they are not a transplant of the first-pair Bernoulli formula. Chebyshev's inequality gives a nonasymptotic sign-error upper bound `(Var_1+Var_0)/(N(Delta mu)^2)` for the two-arm mean estimator. Consequently a sufficiently large constant times

    h_I/(kappa^2 b g^4 A_I^2)

is sufficient for any fixed desired Chebyshev error level in this regime. No Gaussian approximation or optimality assertion is needed. Preparation or baseline-calibration error comparable to Delta mu would require its own control.

For fixed nonzero `A_I/tau_*`, fixed `h_I/tau_*`, and the explicit choices `kappa tau_*=g^(2+alpha)` and `b/tau_*=g^(2+beta)`, this sufficient order is

    N = O(g^(-10-2alpha-beta)),                       (24)

with a sufficiently large constant for the stated fixed error target. This notation gives an available sufficient schedule, not a lower bound. Replacing the rate by the more restrictive `kappa tau_*=g^(8+alpha)` gives the correspondingly larger sufficient order `g^(-22-2alpha-beta)` at fixed graph. Neither exponent establishes feasible preparation, resetting, timing resolution, total energy cost, or observational identification.

